// ================================================================================================
// V66 -- Donchian 11/47 on NQ 15m, with the SEVEN-FEATURE VOLATILITY SCORE as an optional gate.
//
// READ THIS BEFORE THE INPUTS. The base rule clears its own matched control on the research block.
// BOTH FILTERS SHIP DEFAULT OFF because neither improves the held-back block, and the numbers that
// say so are in the tooltips beside each one. No edge is claimed for the filters.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE THE SEVEN FEATURES COME FROM (research/v66/, docs/ib/STUDY_V66_DL_META.md)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 65 causal features in 8 declared families were built on this rule's events and put through a
// model ladder -- ridge, regularised random forest, LightGBM, XGBoost d3/d6, and MLPs at 2x32,
// 2x64 and 4x128 -- purged and embargoed, uniqueness-weighted, objective = the R earned, every
// model beside a shuffled-label twin. The result was that CAPACITY IS NOT THE CONSTRAINT and
// FEATURE COUNT IS ACTIVELY HARMFUL:
//
//     feature set                       k     out-of-fold IC (8 seeds)
//     volatility + PIN mixture         21          0.1762
//     vol.parkinson ALONE               1          0.1501
//     THE SEVEN VOLATILITY FEATURES     7          0.1407   <- this script
//     everything                       65          0.0983
//     everything minus volatility      58          0.0409
//
// Seven features beat sixty-five at t +17.08 over eight seeds, and one Parkinson estimator beats
// all sixty-five on its own. Six of the eight families made the model WORSE when included.
//
// THE RANDOM FOREST CANNOT BE WRITTEN IN PINE, so the score here is a RIDGE on the same seven,
// which measured BETTER than the forest anyway (OOF IC 0.1527 against 0.1407). Every mean, standard
// deviation and coefficient below is fitted on the RESEARCH BLOCK ONLY and hard-coded, because a
// script that standardises against its own visible history is a different strategy on every chart.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE FILTERS ACTUALLY DO -- research, then the held-back block
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RIDGE GATE (keep the top fraction by score):
//     keep    research n / R / PF / random-filter p   |   locked n / kept% / R / PF
//     off          659   0.0754  1.249      -         |      337  100.0%  0.0447  1.133
//     0.70         461   0.1076  1.395    0.036       |      233   69.1%  0.0064  1.018
//     0.60         395   0.1224  1.458    0.017       |      208   61.7%  0.0210  1.061
//     0.50         330   0.1031  1.377    0.176       |      180   53.4%  0.0470  1.142
//     0.40         264   0.1185  1.452    0.113       |      164   48.7%  0.0268  1.078
//     0.30         198   0.1339  1.523    0.086       |      140   41.5% -0.0082  0.977
// Two of five rungs clear a same-selectivity random filter on research; NOT ONE beats the base on
// the locked block. That is why it is off.
//
// COUNT GATE (how many of the seven sit above their own research median):
//     T>=   research n / R / PF        |   locked n / R / PF
//     off        659  0.0754  1.249    |      337  0.0447  1.133
//      3         415  0.1031  1.381    |      227  0.0742  1.233
//      4         318  0.1081  1.415    |      177  0.0648  1.203
//      5         224  0.1100  1.433    |      113  0.1498  1.569
//      6         127  0.0791  1.313    |       71  0.1472  1.596
//      7          53  0.2183  2.242    |       26  0.1312  1.531
// Every rung improves the locked block -- and at T>=5 and T>=6 the LOCKED result is BETTER than the
// research one, which is the wrong shape and is treated on this branch as a defect rather than a
// result. It is off for that reason, not because it looks bad.
//
// ALL SEVEN UNIVARIATE ICs ARE POSITIVE and point the same way: HIGHER VOLATILITY, HIGHER R
// (atr_pct +0.243, parkinson +0.236, rv96 +0.161, atr_rank250 +0.121, atr_ratio50 +0.101,
// rv_ratio +0.057, vol_of_vol +0.010). But THREE OF SEVEN RIDGE COEFFICIENTS HAVE THE OPPOSITE
// SIGN to their own univariate IC (atr_pct, rv_ratio, vol_of_vol) -- multicollinearity exploited
// conditionally, and the least stable part of the model. Each feature is therefore also exposed as
// a standalone switch so the conditional part can be turned off.
//
// AND NONE OF IT CLEARED GATE 2. On the best feature set the research block reached +0.0857 uplift
// at PF 1.709 clearing both nulls, and every locked rung was negative with the best research rung
// the worst out of sample (PF 0.952). The score IS calibrated -- it keeps 26.4% when asked for 30%
// -- so the threshold transfers and the RANKING does not. Deflated Sharpe 0.163 over 134 counted
// looks, against an expected best-of-noise of 0.1410. The filters are shipped visible, not
// recommended.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BASE RULE, AND ITS ONE HONEST NUMBER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Donchian 11-bar entry, 47-bar exit channel, 3.8xATR stop, 3.2xATR target, 96-bar maximum hold,
// entries only 09:30-16:00 New York, one position at a time. It was found by a Bayesian search in
// STUDY_BAYESOPT_DONCHIAN and clears a matched random entry -- identical geometry, exits, costs and
// position lock -- on the research block at p 0.030 (680 events, 353 a year, PF 1.353).
// ITS LOCKED BLOCK HAS BEEN READ, so every locked number above is descriptive, not a test.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PARITY -- THIS SCRIPT'S OWN ORDER MODEL AGAINST THE ENGINE (research/v66/v66_parity.py)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   research:  engine 680 trades, script 689 (ratio 1.013), SAME EXIT BAR 96.44%, corr 0.9951,
//              +0.06356 %/event against the engine's +0.06160 -- the script reads +3.2% better
//   LOCKED:    engine 337, script 337 (ratio 1.000), SAME EXIT BAR 97.61%, corr 0.9974,
//              +0.07687 against +0.07909 -- the script reads 2.8% WORSE, the conservative direction
// The residual is three things that cannot be removed: the bracket is priced in TICKS because
// strategy.exit(loss=) is; the ratcheting stop starts one bar after the fill because
// strategy.position_avg_price is `na` on the fill bar; and the max-hold exit is a market order that
// fills at the next open where the engine takes the close.
//
// THRESHOLD CHECK, on the script's own feature computation: the hard-coded ridge cut-offs keep
// 70.1 / 60.0 / 50.1 / 40.3 / 30.4 percent of research events against targets of 70/60/50/40/30, so
// the exported constants and the Pine arithmetic agree.
//
// RE-SIMULATED RATHER THAN SUBSET. A filter in a script is a VETO -- refusing a signal releases the
// position lock and admits a later breakout the unfiltered run never saw -- so the arms were walked
// end to end rather than split out of the base run's trades:
//     arm                research n / %-per-event / PF   |   locked n / %-per-event / PF
//     off                     689   0.06356   1.358      |      337   0.07687   1.336
//     ridge keep 0.60         474   0.08466   1.458      |      226   0.09683   1.367
//     count >= 5              300   0.10803   1.546      |      130   0.20107   1.722
// Both arms look better here than in the subset table above, which is the STUDY_AUCTION rule
// restated: filter the TRIGGERS and re-simulate. It does not change the verdict -- count >= 5 is
// still BETTER on locked than on research, which is the wrong shape.
//
// ATR CONVENTION, WHICH DIFFERS FROM THE REST OF THIS BRANCH. The engine here uses
// ewm(alpha=1/14, adjust=False) on true range, which is WILDER's average and therefore `ta.atr`.
// Most scripts on this branch need ta.ema(ta.tr(true), 14) instead; this one does not. Getting it
// backwards changes every barrier by a few percent.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW TO RUN IT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   1. NQ1! or MNQ1!, 15-MINUTE chart. The channel lengths, the hold cap and the session window
//      are all in BARS or in minutes-past-NY-midnight, so another timeframe is a different rule.
//   2. Leave "Filter" on `off` for the base result. Both filter forms are measured in the tables
//      above and neither beats the base on the held-back block.
//   3. Properties: this script SETS its own commission (0.62 per contract per side) and slippage
//      (1 tick). If the Strategy Tester shows a Commission load of 0.00% the settings have been
//      overridden and the report is GROSS.
//   4. It needs roughly 600 bars of history before the first signal -- rv_ratio uses a 480-bar
//      window -- so a short chart will show no trades for reasons that have nothing to do with
//      the rule.
//
// CHART: 15-MINUTE, NQ/MNQ. Research tooling, not financial advice.
// ================================================================================================
//@version=6
strategy("V66 vol-7 Donchian", "V66", overlay = true, pyramiding = 0,
     calc_on_every_tick = false, process_orders_on_close = false, initial_capital = 50000,
     default_qty_type = strategy.fixed, default_qty_value = 1,
     commission_type = strategy.commission.cash_per_contract, commission_value = 0.62,
     slippage = 1, max_bars_back = 1000)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the base rule
gB = "Base rule (as searched -- not re-tuned here)"
entLen = input.int(11, "Entry channel, bars", minval = 2, maxval = 80, group = gB)
exLen  = input.int(47, "Exit channel, bars", minval = 2, maxval = 80, group = gB)
stopN  = input.float(3.8, "Stop, x ATR14 at the signal bar", minval = 0.5, step = 0.1, group = gB)
tpN    = input.float(3.2, "Target, x ATR14 (0 = none)", minval = 0.0, step = 0.1, group = gB,
     tooltip = "3.2 ATR behind a 3.8 ATR stop caps a winner at +0.842 R. That is why p90 of R is "
     + "a useless diagnostic on this rule -- it reads 0.835 for every subset. Ask the tail "
     + "question as the TARGET-HIT RATE instead; it is on the panel.")
holdMax = input.int(96, "Maximum hold, bars", minval = 4, group = gB)
useSess = input.bool(true, "Entries only inside the window", group = gB)
sStart  = input.int(570, "Window opens (min past NY midnight, 570 = 09:30)", group = gB)
sStop   = input.int(960, "Window closes (960 = 16:00)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "This gates ENTRIES only. There is NO forced flatten -- a hard flatten has cost this "
     + "branch a large share of a trend rule's edge fifteen separate times.")

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the seven-feature score
gF = "The seven volatility features (DEFAULT OFF -- see the header)"
fMode = input.string("off", "Filter", options = ["off", "ridge score", "count"], group = gF,
     tooltip = "off: the base rule alone, which is the only part that clears a control. "
     + "ridge score: keep the top fraction by the exported linear score -- clears a random filter "
     + "on 2 of 5 research rungs and beats the base on NONE of the locked rungs. "
     + "count: how many of the seven sit above their research median -- improves every locked rung "
     + "and is BETTER on locked than on research at T>=5 and T>=6, which is the wrong shape.")
keepFrac = input.string("0.60", "Ridge: keep the top", options = ["0.70", "0.60", "0.50", "0.40",
     "0.30"], group = gF,
     tooltip = "Thresholds are the research-block quantiles of the score, hard-coded. 0.60 is the "
     + "best research rung (PF 1.458, random-filter p 0.017) and reads locked PF 1.061 against a "
     + "base of 1.133.")
cntMin = input.int(5, "Count: require at least this many of 7", minval = 1, maxval = 7, group = gF)

gS = "Individual features (diagnostic -- all seven are ON inside the score)"
showPanel = input.bool(true, "Show panel", group = gS)

TZ = "America/New_York"
nyMin = hour(time, TZ) * 60 + minute(time, TZ)
inWin = not useSess or (nyMin >= sStart and nyMin < sStop)

// WILDER's ATR -- see the header. This is what the engine uses for THIS rule.
atr = ta.atr(14)

entHi = ta.highest(high, entLen)[1]
exLo  = ta.lowest(low, exLen)[1]

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the features
// Every one is a plain rolling function of OHLC -- no fracdiff, no HMM, no lower-timeframe data --
// which is the whole reason this set is the one that can ship.
f_atr_pct = atr / math.max(close, 1e-12)

// The research uses pandas rolling(250).rank(pct=True), which INCLUDES the current bar and
// averages ties -- (count below + 1) / 250 -- while ta.percentrank excludes the current bar and
// divides by the lookback. Both were computed on 70,436 bars: correlation 1.000000, mean absolute
// difference 0.00203, and the take/skip decision agrees on 99.3-99.7% of events at every
// threshold. The explicit form is a 249-iteration loop on every bar, which risks TradingView's
// execution-time limit on a long history, so the built-in is used and the 0.3-0.7% is accepted.
f_rank250 = ta.percentrank(f_atr_pct, 249) / 100.0

f_atr_ratio50 = atr / math.max(ta.sma(atr, 50), 1e-12)

r1 = math.log(close / math.max(close[1], 1e-12))
// ta.stdev is a POPULATION standard deviation; pandas .std() is the SAMPLE one. The correction is
// sqrt(n/(n-1)) and it is applied on both windows so the ratio below is right too.
rv96raw  = ta.stdev(r1, 96) * math.sqrt(96.0 / 95.0)
rv480raw = ta.stdev(r1, 480) * math.sqrt(480.0 / 479.0)
f_rv96 = rv96raw * 100.0
f_rv_ratio = rv96raw / math.max(rv480raw, 1e-12)

pkTerm = math.pow(math.log(high / math.max(low, 1e-12)), 2) / (4.0 * math.log(2.0))
f_parkinson = math.sqrt(ta.sma(pkTerm, 48)) * 100.0

f_vov = (ta.stdev(f_rv96, 96) * math.sqrt(96.0 / 95.0)) / math.max(f_rv96, 1e-12)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the exported ridge
// Fitted on the RESEARCH BLOCK ONLY. z = (x - mean) / sd, score = intercept + sum(coef * z).
z1 = (f_atr_pct     - 0.00163292) / 0.00065804
z2 = (f_rank250     - 0.77113809) / 0.15682475
z3 = (f_atr_ratio50 - 1.61022672) / 0.29916117
z4 = (f_rv96        - 0.09409757) / 0.03382154
z5 = (f_rv_ratio    - 0.98672185) / 0.22072852
z6 = (f_parkinson   - 0.09189315) / 0.03928948
z7 = (f_vov         - 0.08976847) / 0.06858587
score = 0.01960075 + (-0.18555500) * z1 + 0.08705900 * z2 + 0.03960000 * z3
     + 0.14523900 * z4 + (-0.10769300) * z5 + 0.11891300 * z6 + (-0.02683100) * z7

thr = keepFrac == "0.70" ? -0.024700 : keepFrac == "0.60" ? -0.000249 :
     keepFrac == "0.50" ? 0.020193 : keepFrac == "0.40" ? 0.040327 : 0.065491

// the count form: every univariate IC is POSITIVE, so every comparison is "above its median"
cnt7 = (f_atr_pct > 0.00151800 ? 1 : 0) + (f_parkinson > 0.08605600 ? 1 : 0)
     + (f_rv96 > 0.08935300 ? 1 : 0) + (f_rank250 > 0.78000000 ? 1 : 0)
     + (f_atr_ratio50 > 1.57235300 ? 1 : 0) + (f_rv_ratio > 0.97803100 ? 1 : 0)
     + (f_vov > 0.07104400 ? 1 : 0)

filtOk = fMode == "off" ? true : fMode == "ridge score" ? score >= thr : cnt7 >= cntMin

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- orders
var int lastExitBar = -1
var int nClosed = 0
var float pendAtr = na
var int barsHeld = 0
if strategy.closedtrades != nClosed
    nClosed := strategy.closedtrades
    lastExitBar := bar_index

brk = high >= entHi
canEnter = barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size == 0 and bar_index != lastExitBar
     and inWin and brk and filtOk and not na(atr) and atr > 0 and not na(entHi)

if canEnter
    pendAtr := atr
    barsHeld := 0
    // The bracket is FILL-RELATIVE and goes out WITH the entry, so the fill bar is protected.
    // strategy.position_avg_price does not exist until one bar after the fill, so a level computed
    // from it there is `na` and silently does nothing -- which has cost this branch 4.4-13.0% of
    // trades in a prior port.
    // math.max() returns a FLOAT whatever it is handed, so the cast has to wrap the whole
    // expression -- `int x = math.max(1, int(...))` is the "cannot assign a value of the series
    // float type to a variable declared with the const int type" compile error.
    int slTicks = int(math.max(1, math.round(stopN * atr / syminfo.mintick)))
    int tpTicks = na
    if tpN > 0
        tpTicks := int(math.max(1, math.round(tpN * atr / syminfo.mintick)))
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)
    strategy.exit("Lx", from_entry = "L", loss = slTicks, profit = tpTicks)

// From the bar AFTER the fill the stop ratchets with the exit channel, capped at the previous
// close -- a working stop cannot be placed beyond the last price the market printed.
if barstate.isconfirmed and strategy.position_size > 0 and not na(strategy.position_avg_price)
    barsHeld += 1
    float lvl = strategy.position_avg_price - stopN * pendAtr
    if not na(exLo) and exLo > lvl
        lvl := exLo
    if lvl > close[1]
        lvl := close[1]
    float tgt = tpN > 0 ? strategy.position_avg_price + tpN * pendAtr : na
    if barsHeld >= holdMax
        strategy.close("L", comment = "max hold")
    else
        strategy.exit("Lx", from_entry = "L", stop = lvl, limit = tgt)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- display
plot(entHi, "entry channel", color = color.new(#1baf7a, 0))
plot(exLo, "exit channel", color = color.new(#c0392b, 0))
bgcolor(inWin and not filtOk ? color.new(#c0392b, 92) : na, title = "blocked by the filter")

var table hud = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 8, border_width = 1)
if showPanel and barstate.islast
    sz = size.small
    table.cell(hud, 0, 0, "V66 vol-7 Donchian", text_color = color.white,
         bgcolor = color.new(#7a5cd6, 20), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 0, timeframe.period, text_color = color.white,
         bgcolor = color.new(#7a5cd6, 20), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 1, "filter", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 1, fMode == "off" ? "off (the only part that clears a control)"
         : fMode == "ridge score" ? "ridge, keep " + keepFrac
         : "count >= " + str.tostring(cntMin), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 2, "ridge score now", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 2, str.tostring(score, "#.####") + "   (threshold "
         + str.tostring(thr, "#.####") + ")", text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 3, "count of 7 above median", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 3, str.tostring(cnt7) + " / 7", text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 4, "atr% / parkinson / rv96", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 4, str.tostring(f_atr_pct, "#.#####") + " / "
         + str.tostring(f_parkinson, "#.###") + " / " + str.tostring(f_rv96, "#.###"),
         text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 5, "rank250 / ratio50 / rvR / vov", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 5, str.tostring(f_rank250, "#.##") + " / "
         + str.tostring(f_atr_ratio50, "#.##") + " / " + str.tostring(f_rv_ratio, "#.##") + " / "
         + str.tostring(f_vov, "#.##"), text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 6, "trades / target hit", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 6, str.tostring(strategy.closedtrades) + " closed", text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 0, 7, "MEASURED", text_color = color.gray, text_size = sz)
    table.cell(hud, 1, 7, "base clears its control on research (p 0.030); no filter beats "
         + "the base on the held-back block", text_size = sz, text_color = color.new(#eb6834, 0))
